Coulomb's inverse-square law, or simply Coulomb's law, is a scientific law of physics that describes the amount of force between two electrically charged particales at rest. 
This electirc force is conventionally called the electrostatic force or Coulomb force. 
Although the law was known earlier, it was first published in 1785 by French physicist Charles-Augustin de Coulomb. 
Coulomb's law was essential to the development of the theory of electromagnetism and may even be its starting point, as it allowed meaningful discussions of the amount of electric charge in a particle
